# nr-relay-feedback-core: wire lines for mixdog-relay (src/feedback: validation, durable records, delivery schedule, attachments)

No protocol method is added and nothing is registered in a hot file.

## native/core/crates/mixdog-relay/Cargo.toml

After the existing `[[test]]` block of `twin` add (the tests use the `faults()` hook and the scripted rig of the `test-support` feature):

    [[test]]
    name = "feedback_service"
    path = "tests/feedback_service.rs"
    required-features = ["test-support"]

No dependency is added: the code uses base64, sha2, serde_json, mixdog-js, mixdog-rt, mixdog-fsio and tokio (rt, sync, macros)
that the manifest already lists; the tests use tempfile and tokio test-util from [dev-dependencies]. Cargo.lock and the workspace
hack need nothing.

## native/core/Cargo.toml / compose_flip.rs / protocol tables

Nothing (no SERVICE_PENDING name is removed: the queue is not a protocol method).

## What the later tasks connect

nr-relay-feedback-http and nr-relay-server use this (all in `mixdog_relay::feedback`):

- `FeedbackService::start(&data_dir, tokens.durability_io, sender: Option<Arc<dyn FeedbackSender>>, FeedbackOptions)` (async): creates
  `<data_dir>/feedback` (0700) even without a sender, loads the records, starts the delivery task on `mixdog_rt::spawn`. The
  process must have installed the mixdog-rt backend (`exec::boot`) and hand over `LaneTokens::durability_io`.
- `FeedbackOptions { from, to, now, log, .. }`: `from` is SMTP_USER and `to` is FEEDBACK_TO (default support@mixdog.ai) when mail is
  configured; the defaults of `FEEDBACK_DEFAULTS` are `FeedbackOptions::default()`. `smtpTimeoutMs` belongs to the mail transport
  (src/mail), not to the queue.
- `trait FeedbackSender`: `send(FeedbackMail) -> Pin<Box<dyn Future<Output = Result<(), SendFailure>> + Send>>` and `close()`;
  `SendFailure.code` is the nodemailer-style code that the line `[relay] feedback send failed id= code=` prints. The mailer of
  nr-relay-feedback-http converts `FeedbackMail` (strings are parsed text: encode with `js::node_utf8`) into a src/mail message.
- `check_rate(ip)`, `submit(&Value) -> Result<Accepted, FeedbackError{status, code}>`, `configured()`, `close()`; `MAX_BODY_BYTES`.
  The HTTP layer answers `error.status` and the body `{"error": error.code}`, `202 {id, status:"accepted"}` for `Accepted`.

## Fixtures

New, added with `git add --sparse`: native/core/fixtures/relay-feedback-records/records.json (what the Node service wrote, recorded)
and record.mjs (the recorder: `node record.mjs --tree <checkout>`; read only on the tree, writes records.json beside itself).
It is not in native/core/fixtures/MANIFEST.json (that index is for the P0-BENCH sets and carries a checksum the lead computes).

## What this task could not compile

cargo was not run (the lead compiles the wave). The sources were only syntax-checked with rustfmt.
